<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Argentina</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Argentina is a country in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a> that emerged from the Spanish viceroyalty of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-la-plata">Río de la Plata</a>. In the nineteenth century it was a rival of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazilian-empire">Brazilian Empire</a> for influence in the Río de la Plata region, notably in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cisplatine-war">Cisplatine War</a>, and later an ally in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paraguayan-war">Paraguayan War</a>. Its economy was closely tied to British investment and trade.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/argentina.jpg" alt="Argentina" width="320" height="200" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Manuel Belgrano · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Argentina.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the national census of 1914 about 30 per cent of <b>Argentina</b>’s population had been born abroad, twice the share in the United States at the time.</blockquote><a href="/empire/argentine-republic">Argentine Republic · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Brazil bore the overwhelming burden of the conflict — <b>Argentina</b> and Uruguay contributed forces, but the Brazilian army and navy did the great majority of the fighting across six years of extraordinarily costly campaigns against a Paraguayan resistance that fought on even as its country was devastated.</blockquote><a href="/empire/brazilian-empire">Brazilian Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The City of London was the world's pre-eminent financial centre through the nineteenth century, sterling under the gold standard served as the principal international currency, and British capital financed railways from <b>Argentina</b> to India to Australia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/british-empire">British Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Túpac Inca Yupanqui defeated the Kingdom of Chimor around 1470 and campaigned south through Collasuyu into present-day Chile and north-west <b>Argentina</b>, where the advance stopped near the Maule River in the face of Mapuche resistance.</blockquote><a href="/empire/inca-empire">Inca Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Poverty drove mass emigration: between the 1870s and 1914 well over ten million Italians left, many for the United States, <b>Argentina</b> and Brazil, and their remittances supported families at home.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Cattle, introduced in 1603, have been its wealth ever since: after the Guerra Grande livestock exports rose steeply, Montevideo became a financial centre and entrepôt for <b>Argentina</b>, Brazil and Paraguay, and Italian and Spanish immigrants swelled the population from under 75,000 at independence to over 438,500 by 1879.</blockquote><a href="/empire/oriental-republic-of-uruguay">Oriental Republic of Uruguay · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
